<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Review through Section 3.4</title>
  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>


<page>
  <activity xml:id="act-sinusoid-graphs">
  <title>Trig Practice</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Each of the graphs below shows a function of the form
      <m>{y = a\sin(k(t-b)) + c}</m>. All graphs use the same scales
      so you can compare them directly.
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="25% 25% 25%" margins="0%">
      <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of a sinusoidal function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-review-3-4a">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=3*sin(pi/2*(t-1))+1</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-6,8,6)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes"  xlabel="t" ylabel="y"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(4,4)">Graph A</label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

      <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of a sinusoidal function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-review-3-4b">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=2*sin(pi*(t-0.5))-1</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-6,8,6)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes"  xlabel="t" ylabel="y"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(4,4)">Graph B</label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>


      <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of a sinusoidal function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-review-3-4c">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=1*sin(pi/3*(t-2))+2</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-6,8,6)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes"  xlabel="t" ylabel="y"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(4,4)">Graph C</label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <sidebyside widths="25% 25% 25%" margins="0%">
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of a sinusoidal function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-review-3-4d">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=4*sin(pi/4*(t-0))+0</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-6,8,6)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes"  xlabel="t" ylabel="y"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(4,4)">Graph D</label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of a sinusoidal function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-review-3-4e">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=2*sin(pi/2*(t+1))+3</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-6,8,6)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes"  xlabel="t" ylabel="y"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(4,5.3)">Graph E</label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of a sinusoidal function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-review-3-4f">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=3*sin(pi*(t-1))-2</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-6,8,6)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes"  xlabel="t" ylabel="y"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(4,5.3)">Graph F</label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

    </sidebyside>
  </introduction>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which graph has the greatest amplitude? Which has the smallest amplitude?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        The amplitude is half the distance from the minimum to the maximum.
        Graph D runs from <m>-4</m> to <m>4</m>, so its amplitude is 4.  This is 
        the largest amplitude.
        Graph C runs from <m>1</m> to <m>3</m>, so its amplitude is 1, which is the smallest 
        amplitude.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which graph(s) have an amplitude of 3?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
       Graph A and F.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which graph(s) have the longest period? Which graph(s) have the shortest period?
      </p>
    </statement>
   
    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        Longest: D (period 8). Shortest: B and F (period 2).

        The period is the horizontal length of one full cycle.
        Graph D completes exactly one cycle on <m>[0,8]</m>, so its period is 8.
        Graphs B and F complete four cycles on <m>[0,8]</m>, so their period is 2.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which graph(s) have a period of 4?
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        Graphs A and E.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which graph(s) have the midline <m>y = 0</m>? Which graph(s) have the highest midline?
        Which have the lowest midline?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        The midline is halfway between the maximum and minimum.
        The midlines are: A, <m>y=1</m>; B, <m>y=-1</m>; C, <m>y=2</m>;
        D, <m>y=0</m>; E, <m>y=3</m>; F, <m>y=-2</m>. So Graph D has the 
        highest midline, and graph F has the lowest.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find two pairs of graphs that have the same amplitude but different periods.
      </p>
    </statement>
 
    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        B and E (amplitude 2, periods 2 and 4); A and F (amplitude 3, periods 4 and 2).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which graph(s) are concave up on the interval <m>(4,8)</m>? Which graph(s) are increasing on <m>(2,4)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        A sine function (and cosine function) is concave up exactly where it lies below its midline
        (it's bending upward!).
        Graph D is concave up on the whole interval from <m>(4,8)</m>.  Others such 
        as A and C are only concave up on part of that interval.
      
      </p>
      <p>
        On <m>(2,4)</m>, Graph E goes from its lowest point (minimum) at <m>t=2</m> up to its highest point (maximum)
        at <m>t=4</m>, so it is increasing the whole time.
        Others are not increasing over that whole interval.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Choose 1+ of the graphs and write a possible formula of the form
        <m>{y = a\sin(k(t-b)) + c}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        The amplitude is almost <m>a</m>, though <m>a</m> could be positive or negative 
        depending on if it looks like sine or flipped sine.  The midline tells 
        us the value of <m>c</m>.  The value of <m>k</m> is <m>\frac{2\pi}{Period}</m>. 
        Finally the value of <m>b</m> comes from a horizontal shift to find the anchor point. 
        All of these could be rewritten with different values of <m>b</m> (and a possible 
        sign change of the value of <m>a</m>), but here are the <q>simplest</q> formulas (i.e. the 
        first positive anchor point is the most common choice from my experience).
     
      </p>
   
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>A: <m>y = 3\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2}(t-1)\right) + 1</m></p></li>
          <li><p>B: <m>y = 2\sin\left(\pi(t-0.5)\right) - 1</m></p></li>
          <li><p>C: <m>y = \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}(t-2)\right)+2</m></p></li>
          <li><p>D: <m>y = 4\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}t\right)</m></p></li>
          <li><p>E: <m>y = -2\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2}(t-1)\right) + 3</m></p></li>
          <li><p>F: <m>y = -3\sin\left(\pi t\right) - 2</m></p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>
</page>

<page>
<activity>
<introduction>
  <p>
    Practice finding exponential functions.
  </p>
</introduction>
  <task workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the equation for the exponential function of the form <m>{p(t) = ab^t}</m>
        that goes through the points:
        <m>{(4,10)}</m> and <m>{(8,2)}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        Divide the two equations: <m>\dfrac{ab^8}{ab^4} = \dfrac{2}{10}</m>, so <m>b^4 = \dfrac{1}{5}</m>
        and <m>b = \left(\dfrac{1}{5}\right)^{1/4}</m>.
        Then <m>ab^4 = 10</m> gives <m>a\cdot\dfrac{1}{5} = 10</m>, so <m>a = 50</m>. 
        So <m>{p(t) = 50\left(\frac{1}{5}\right)^{t/4}}</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the equation for the exponential function of the form <m>{p(t) = ab^t}</m>
        that goes through the points:
        <m>{(4.4,1.2)}</m> and <m>{(10.4,9)}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        Divide the two equations: <m>\dfrac{ab^{10.4}}{ab^{4.4}} = \dfrac{9}{1.2}</m>,
        so <m>b^6 = 7.5</m> and <m>b = 7.5^{1/6}\approx 1.399</m>.
        Then <m>ab^{4.4} = 1.2</m> gives
        <m>a = \dfrac{1.2}{7.5^{4.4/6}} \approx 0.274</m>. So 
        <m>{p(t)= \dfrac{1.2}{7.5^{4.4/6}} (7.5^{1/6})^t\approx 0.274(1.399)^t}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>
</page>
<page>
<activity>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Solve the following exactly:
    </p>

    <p>


<ol cols="2" marker="a.">
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>10^x = 70</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>10^{2x} = 300</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>4 \cdot 10^x = 60</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>10^{x-2} = 35</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>3 \cdot 10^{4x} = 21</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>10^{-x} = 0.2</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>6 \cdot 10^{2x+1} = 240</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>10^{5x} = 1</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\log x = 2.5</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\log(4x) = 3</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>2\log x = 9</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>3\log(2x) = 12</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\log x = -1.5</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>4\log(3x+1) + 2 = 14</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\log(x^2) = \log 25</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\log(5x) = 0</m></p></li>
</ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
    <p>
      Note that <m>\log(x) = \log_{10}(x)</m>.
    </p>
    <ol cols="2" marker="a.">
      <li><p>
        <m>x = \log 70</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>2x = \log 300</m>, so <m>x = \dfrac{\log 300}{2}</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>10^x = 15</m>, so <m>x = \log 15</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>x - 2 = \log 35</m>, so <m>x = 2 + \log 35</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>10^{4x} = 7</m>, so <m>x = \dfrac{\log 7}{4}</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>-x = \log 0.2</m>, so <m>x = -\log 0.2 = \log 5</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>10^{2x+1} = 40</m>, so <m>2x+1 = \log 40</m> and
        <m>x = \dfrac{\log 40 - 1}{2}</m>. Since <m>\log 40 = 1 + \log 4</m>,
        this simplifies to <m>x = \dfrac{\log 4}{2} = \log 2</m>.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>5x = \log 1 = 0</m>, so <m>x = 0</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>x = 10^{2.5}</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>4x = 10^3</m>, so <m>x = 250</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>\log x = 4.5</m>, so <m>x = 10^{4.5}</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>\log(2x) = 4</m>, so <m>2x = 10^4</m> and <m>x = 5000</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>x = 10^{-1.5} </m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>\log(3x+1) = 3</m>, so <m>3x+1 = 1000</m> and <m>x = 333</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>x^2 = 25</m>, so <m>x = \pm 5</m>. Both values are ok,
        since <m>x^2 = 25 \gt 0</m> in each case and so we still have the log of a positive number.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>5x = 10^0 = 1</m>, so <m>x = \dfrac{1}{5}</m>
      </p></li>
    </ol>
  </solution>

        </activity>

        </page>

<page>

<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A bacteria population grows according to <m>P(t) = 1000 \cdot 10^{0.5t}</m>,
      where <m>t</m> is time in hours.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the initial population.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        <m>P(0) = 1000\cdot 10^0 = 1000</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the population after 2 hours.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        <m>P(2) = 1000\cdot 10^{0.5\cdot 2} = 1000\cdot 10 = 10,000</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the exact time when the population is 3000.  (There should be a log in your answer.)
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        Solve <m>1000\cdot 10^{0.5t} = 3000</m>. Dividing by 1000 gives <m>10^{0.5t} = 3</m>.
        Then <m>0.5t = \log 3</m>, so <m>t = 2\log 3</m> hours (this is approximately <m>0.954</m> hours).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A radioactive substance in grams decays according to <m>A(t) = 500 \cdot 10^{kt}</m>,
      where <m>t</m> is time in years and <m>k</m> is some unknown number.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If after 5 years there was 250 grams of the substance, what is <m>k</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        Plug in <m>t=5</m> and the output <m>250</m>. Solve <m>250 = 500\cdot 10^{5k}</m>. Then <m>\frac{1}{2}  = 10^{5k}</m>,
        so <m>5k = \log\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right) </m>
        and <m>k = \dfrac15\log(\frac12)</m> (which is approximately <m>-0.0602</m>).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      An investment grows according to <m>A(t) = 5000 \cdot 10^{0.04t}</m>,
      where <m>t</m> is time in years.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        When will the investment double?
      </p>
    </statement>
 
    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        Doubling means <m>A(t) = 10,000</m>. So we are solving <m>10000 = 5000\cdot 10^{0.04t}</m>,
        which simplifies
        to get <m>2 = 10^{0.04t}</m>. Then <m>\log(2) = 0.04t</m>,
        so <m>t = \frac{1}{0.04}\log(2) = 25\log(2)</m> years.  (This is approximately <m>7.53</m>.)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>


<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The intensity of an earthquake is measured as <m>I(M) = I_0 \cdot 10^M</m>,
      where <m>M</m> is the magnitude and <m>I_0</m> is some unknown initial intensity.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace = "1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the intensity of a magnitude 6 earthquake and of a magnitude 4
        earthquake in terms of <m>I_0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        Substitute <m>M=6</m> and <m>M=4</m> into <m>I(M) = I_0\cdot 10^M</m>. Thus we 
        get:
        <m>I(6) = I_0\cdot 10^6</m> and <m>I(4) = I_0\cdot 10^4</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How many times more intense is a magnitude 6 earthquake than a magnitude 4 earthquake?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{I(6)}{I(4)} = \dfrac{I_0\cdot 10^6}{I_0\cdot 10^4} = 10^2 = 100</m>.  So 100 times bigger.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

</page>

<page>

<activity>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Solve the following exactly.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol cols="2" marker="a.">
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>e^x = 20</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>e^{2x} = 50</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>3e^x = 45</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>e^{x-1} = 10</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>2e^{3x} = 14</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>e^{-x} = 0.5</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>5e^{2x+1} = 100</m></p></li>

  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>e^{3x} = 1</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\ln x = 3</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\ln(2x) = 5</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>3\ln x = 12</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>2\ln(3x) = 8</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\ln x = -0.5</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>5\ln(2x+1) +5= 15</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\ln(x^2) = \ln 9</m></p></li>
  <li workspace="1cm"><p><m>\ln(3x) = 0</m></p></li>
</ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
    <ol cols="2" marker="a.">
      <li><p>
        <m>x = \ln 20</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>2x = \ln 50</m>, so <m>x = \dfrac{1}{2}\ln(50)</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>e^x = 15</m>, so <m>x = \ln(15)</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>x - 1 = \ln(10)</m>, so <m>x = 1 + \ln(10)</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>e^{3x} = 7</m>, so <m>x = \dfrac{1}{3}\ln(7)</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>-x = \ln 0.5</m>, so <m>x = -\ln(0.5)</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>e^{2x+1} = 20</m>, so <m>2x + 1 = \ln(20)</m> and
        <m>x = \dfrac{\ln(20) - 1}{2}</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>3x = \ln(1) = 0</m>, so <m>x = 0</m> (cool)
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>x = e^3</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>2x = e^5</m>, so <m>x = \dfrac{1}{2}e^5</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>\ln x = 4</m>, so <m>x = e^4</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>\ln(3x) = 4</m>, so <m>3x = e^4</m> and <m>x = \dfrac{1}{3}e^4</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>x = e^{-0.5}</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>5\ln(2x+1) = 10</m>, so <m>\ln(2x+1) = 2</m>,
        <m>2x + 1 = e^2</m>, and <m>x = \dfrac{e^2 - 1}{2}</m>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>x^2 = 9</m>, so <m>x = \pm 3</m>. Both values work since we would be 
        taking the log of a positive number in each case.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <m>3x = e^0 = 1</m>, so <m>x = \dfrac{1}{3}</m>
      </p></li>
    </ol>
  </solution>
</activity>

</page>


<page>

        <activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A cooling object follows <m>T(t) = 20 + 80 \cdot e^{-0.23t}</m>,
      where <m>T</m> is temperature in Celsius and <m>t</m> is time in minutes.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the initial temperature of the object?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        <m>T(0) = 20 + 80e^0 = 100</m> degrees.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the temperature after 10 minutes?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        <m>T(10) = 20 + 80e^{-0.23\cdot 10} = 20 + 80e^{-2.3}</m> degrees. This is
        actually the exact answer.  It turns out to be approximately
         <m>T(10)\approx 28.02</m> degrees.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What will the temperature approach in the long term?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        As <m>t\to\infty</m>, <m>e^{-0.23t}\to 0</m>, so <m>T(t)\to 20 + 0 = 20</m>.
        That is, in the long term it will tend towards the horizontal asymptote of <m>y=20</m> degrees.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The population of a city grows according to <m>P(t) = 10000e^{0.03t}</m>,
      where <m>t</m> is time in years.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the initial population of the city.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        <m>P(0) = 10000e^0 = 10,000</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the population after 10 years?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        <m>P(10) = 10000e^{0.03\cdot 10} = 10000e^{0.3}</m>.  We 
        would stop here for an exact answer, but this is approximately 
        <m>13,499</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How long until the population reaches 30,000?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        We are looking for <m>t</m> when <m>P(t) = 30000</m>, so we 
        solve <m>30000 = 10000e^{0.03t}</m> for <m>t</m>. Then this 
        simplifies to <m>3 = e^{0.03t}</m>,
        so <m>0.03t = \ln(3)</m> and
        <m>t = \dfrac{1}{0.03}\ln(3)</m> years (exactly). This is approximately 
        <m>36.6</m> years.  
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>
</page>

<page>
<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A radioactive element decays according to <m>N(t) = N_0 e^{kt}</m>,
      where <m>t</m> is time in days and <m>N_0, k</m> are unknown.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If there is initially 200 grams, find one of the unknowns.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        We know 
        <m>N(0)=200</m> from the data point, but from the model we have <m>N(0) = N_0e^0 = N_0</m>, 
        so the initial amount is <m>N_0 = 200</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If after 7 days there is 150 grams, find the other unknown.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        So we plug in <m>t=7</m> and <m>N(7) = 150</m>.  Thus we solve 
        <m>150 = 200e^{7k}</m>. Then <m>\frac34 = e^{7k}</m>,
        so <m>7k = \ln\left(\dfrac{3}{4}\right)</m> and
        <m>k = \frac17\ln(\frac34)</m>.  This is the exact value. 
        (This is approximately <m>k\approx -0.0411</m>.)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Compound interest is calculated as <m>A(t) = 1000e^{0.05t}</m> where <m>t</m> is time in years.

    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the initial amount you invest?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        <m>A(0) = 1000e^0 = 1000</m>.  ($1000)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How long until the initial amount doubles?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S3-4-review-soln">
      <p>
        If it doubles we'll have 2000, so <m>A(t) = 2000</m>. So we solve 
        <m>2000 = 1000e^{0.05t}</m>,
        which simplifies to <m>2 = e^{0.05t}</m>. Then <m>0.05t = \ln(2)</m>,
        so <m>t = \frac{1}{0.05}\ln(2)= 20\ln(2)</m> years.  (Exact).  
        This is approximately <m>13.9</m> years!
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

 </page>

</handout>
